---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevEncodeFancyText from '@/components/tools/text/DevEncodeFancyText.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="upside-down-text">
  <DevEncodeFancyText
    client:load
    sample="flip this text"
    featured={['upside-down', 'mirrored', 'spaced']}
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How upside-down text is made</h2>
    <p>
      Upside-down text needs two steps. First each character is replaced with a Unicode look-alike that
      appears rotated 180 degrees. Then the whole string is reversed, so that the words read left to right
      when you turn the page over.
    </p>
    <pre><code>hello
step 1 (substitute):  ɥǝllo
step 2 (reverse):     ollǝɥ</code></pre>
    <p>
      Without the second step, the characters look upside down but read backwards.
    </p>

    <h2>Where the rotated characters come from</h2>
    <p>
      Unicode has no &ldquo;upside-down alphabet&rdquo;. The characters come from wherever a suitable shape
      exists: mostly the International Phonetic Alphabet, and a few from Latin extensions, Greek, Cyrillic and
      Deseret:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Letter</th><th>Flipped</th><th>What it actually is</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>a</td><td>ɐ</td><td>IPA turned A</td></tr>
          <tr><td>e</td><td>ǝ</td><td>Turned E, the schwa</td></tr>
          <tr><td>g</td><td>ƃ</td><td>B with a topbar, from an African orthography</td></tr>
          <tr><td>t</td><td>ʇ</td><td>IPA turned T</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A</td><td>∀</td><td>The mathematical &ldquo;for all&rdquo; symbol</td></tr>
          <tr><td>B</td><td>𐐒</td><td>A Deseret alphabet letter</td></tr>
          <tr><td>E</td><td>Ǝ</td><td>Turned E, used in Pan-Nigerian orthography</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>The letters that will not flip</h2>
    <p>
      Some letters are symmetrical and need no substitute: <code>o</code>, <code>x</code>, <code>z</code>,
      <code>H</code>, <code>I</code>, <code>N</code>, <code>O</code>, <code>S</code>, <code>X</code>,
      <code>Z</code>. Others have no rotated form in Unicode; capital <code>Q</code> and <code>G</code> are the
      usual examples and stay unchanged. This is a limit of the character set.
    </p>

    <h2>Punctuation flips too</h2>
    <p>
      Full stops move to the top of the line, commas become apostrophes, and question and exclamation marks
      become the inverted Spanish forms ¿ and ¡. Brackets and braces change direction, so a pair still encloses
      the right text after reversal.
    </p>

    <h2>Flipped versus reversed versus mirrored</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Flipped: substituted and reversed; reads correctly when you rotate the screen.</li>
      <li>Reversed: the same characters in the opposite order, with no substitution. Useful for palindrome checks.</li>
      <li>Mirrored: a true left-right mirror needs other glyph shapes, and Unicode has far fewer of those, so real mirror text is normally done with CSS.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The tool above has the first two, so you can compare them.</p>

    <h2>Font and device support</h2>
    <p>
      Support is good, because almost every system font has IPA characters. The Deseret capital B is the
      exception: it is outside the Basic Multilingual Plane and shows as a box on some older Android versions.
      All other characters render on current phones and desktops.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
